Abstract
While zirconium $(\mathrm{Zr})$ -based metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising for conversion of levulinic acid (LA) to $\gamma$ -valerolactone (GVL) through catalytic transform hydrogenation (CTH), these reported Zr MOFs for LA conversion must be synthesized in toxic dimethylformamide (DMF). From the viewpoint of sustainability, it is preferable to avoid usage of DMF-based solvents to prepare these Zr MOFs. As water is a green solvent, the aim of this study is to develop and investigate Zr MOFs, which are prepared in water, for LA conversion to GVL. Specifically, monocarboxylic acids (e.g., formic acid, acetic acid and propanoic acid) are employed as modulators during the preparation of water-born ZrF. The role of modulators is extremely important for the well-developed formation of water-born ZrF. In addition, different monocarboxylic acid modulators also significantly influence the morphology of water-born ZrF; nevertheless, their crystalline structures and acidities are equivalent. As for LA conversion, these water-born modulated ZrF MOFs are validated to successfully convert LA to GVL. Especially, the formic acid-modulated ZrF can exhibit LA conversion $=96\%$ , selectivity for $\mathrm{GVL}=98\%$ and yield of GVL $=98\%$ . These water-born modulated ZrF also exhibit even higher catalytic activities than the typical DMF-based ZrF and reported $\mathrm{z}_{\mathrm{r}}$ -based MOFs in LA conversion to GVL. These water-born ZrF could be also reused even without regeneration for multiple cyclic LA conversion. These results and findings prove that the water-born ZrF is not only environmentally benign but also more effective for LA conversion to GVL.
Keywords: levulinic acid, $\gamma$ -valerolactone, Zr fumarate, MOF-801, modulators
## 1. Introduction
Lignocellulosic biomass represents an abundant renewable source, which receives increasing attention as valorization of biomass-derived molecules into valuable products (e.g., fuels and chemicals) constitutes an important step towards sustainability of resources and environment [1, 2]. Specifically, an important biomass-derived levulinic acid (LA) can be hydrogenated to produce a versatile platform chemical, $\gamma$ -valerolactone (GVL) [3, 4], which is a widely-employed additive in fuels [5], and a precursor for many fine chemicals [6-8].
Typically, hydrogenation of LA is accomplished using noble metal catalysts with molecular $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ [9-11]; unfortunately, high cost of noble metals and complication of using high-pressure $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ poses serious challenges on large-scale implementation of this process. On the other hand, hydrogenation of LA can be also achieved via an alternative process called “catalytic transfer hydrogenation (CTH). In contrast to conventional hydrogenation using $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas/noble metals, CTH reactions can be processed just using common alcohols as hydrogen donors and non-noble metal catalysts under relatively mild conditions [12-15].
To date, several non-noble metals have been developed for LA conversion to GVL via CTH, such as nickel, zirconium $(\mathrm{Zr})$ and iron [16-19]. Among these metals, Zr is considered as the most promising non-noble metallic element for LA conversion as Zr possesses acidities which are favorable for generation of GVL from LA [14, 20]. Therefore, a number of studies using $\mathrm{zr}$ -based catalysts have been reported, including $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ -immoblized silica, and $\mathrm{Zr}$ -containing zeolites [3, 14, 21-24]. In addition to these conventional $Z\mathrm{r}$ materials, recently $\mathrm{zr}$ -based metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have also received great attention for LA conversion to GVL as MOFs and CPs because the coordination between $Z\mathrm{r}$ and oxygenic ligands formulates 3-D Zr-O clusters bound to organic ligands, making the resulting MOFs highly hierarchical and porous with high surface areas [12, 25]. For instance, the most common Zr MOF, UiO-66, has been employed for LA conversion via CTH. To enhance LA conversion efficiency to GVL, UiO-66 had been further modified to include sulfonate groups for increasing its acidity [12]. Nevertheless, the conversion efficiencies of LA to GVL by UiO-66 or its acid-functional analogue $\mathrm{\DeltaUiO-66-SO_{3}H},$ are quite limited and an elongated reaction is usually required [12]. For instance, UiO-66-SO3H could only achieve $69\%$ conversion and $25\%$ GVL yield when 2-butanol (2-BuOH) was used as the alcohol during CTH [26].
In addition to UiO-66, another MOF, comprised of Zr and cyanuric acid (CA), has been also prepared and evaluated for LA conversion to GVL [13]. While ZrCA seemed promising for LA conversion to GVL, we find that ZrCA as well as the afore-mentioned UiO-66 or UiO-66-SO3H are prepared using toxic dimethylformamide (DMF) as a solvent. Sometimes, other toxicants, such as triethylamine, must be also added to afford well-developed structures [13]. From the viewpoint of sustainability, it is certainly preferable to avoid usage of DMF-based solvents to prepare these Zr MOFs, especially for generation of fine chemicals from biomass-derived molecules. Nevertheless, very few studies have ever developed Zr-based MOFs, which are prepared using greener solvents, for LA conversion to GVL via CTH [26].
As water is certainly a green solvent, it would be interesting and advantageous to develop water-born Zr-based MOFs for LA conversion. Thus, the aim of this study is to develop and investigate Zr MOFs, which are prepared in water, for LA conversion to GVL. In particular, a special Zr MOF, consisting of $\mathrm{zr}$ and fumarate, is selected as this $\mathrm{zr}$ -fumarate $(\mathrm{ZrF})$ can be synthesized in water. However, if only water is used, the formation of $Z\mathrm{rF}$ may be problematic [27]. Thus, addition of monocarboxylic acids during syntheses of water-born ZrF as modulators has been an important strategy for obtaining well-defined water-born ZrF [27]. As monocarboxylic acid, with various lengths of alkyl chains, have been shown to influence the formation of ZrF as well as the morphology of ZrF [27], it is crucial to investigate catalytic activities of $Z\mathrm{rF}$ prepared with different modulators for LA conversion. To our best knowledge, almost no studies have been conducted to examine roles/effects of modulators for preparing water-born $Z\mathrm{rF}$ on physical and chemical properties as well as catalytic characteristics which influence LA conversion to GVL.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Chemicals employed in this study were all reagent-grade and used directly without purification. $\mathrm{zrCl}_{4}$ , formic acid, propanoic acid, fumaric acid, methyl levulinate (ML), ethyl levulinate (EL), butyl levulinate (BL) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (USA). Levulinic acid (LA), dimethylformamide (DMF) and dimethylacetamide were obtained from Alfa Aesar. Acetic acid, GVL, 1-propanol (1-PrOH), 1-butanol (1-BuOH) and 2-BuOH were received from Acros organics (USA). Methanol, ethanol, isopropanol (2-propanol or 2-PrOH), acetic acid and formic acid were purchased from Union Fine Chemicals, Taiwan. Deionized (DI) water was prepared to less than 18 MΩ-cm.
## 2.2 Preparation and characterizations of Zr-fumarate MOFs
Water-born ZrF MOFs were prepared in water in the presence of various monocarboxylic acid modulators as follows [28]. $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $(2.6~\mathrm{mmol})$ was firstly added to $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of DI water, followed by addition of a monocarboxylic acid (e.g., formic acid) $\cdot182\ \mathrm{\mmol})$ , and 7.8 mmol of fumaric acid. The resulting mixture was subsequently transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel vessel, which was placed inside a conventional oven at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 hours. The corresponding precipitate was then collected and washed with DI water and ethanol thoroughly, and then dried at room temperature to afford water-bore ZrF MOFs. Formic acid-modulated ZrF was abbreviated as F-ZrF, whereas acetic acid-modulated ZrF and propanoic acid-modulated ZrF were abbreviated as A-ZrF and P-ZrF, respectively.
A water-born modulator-free ZrF was also prepared (denoted as W-ZrF) according to the above-mentioned procedures without the addition of any monocarboxylic acids. For comparisons, solvothermally-prepared ZrF in DMF-based solvents was also synthesized according to a previous study without modulators [29]. The precipitate was collected via centrifugation and washed with ethanol and dried to afford DMF-based ZrF, which was designated as D-ZrF.
The morphologies of resulting ZrF MOFs were visualized using scanning electronic microscopy (JEOL, Japan). The size analyses for ZrF particles were conducted by using the image processing software “Image J “ developed at the National Institute of Health to determine sizes of ZrF particles based on SEM images. The sizes were then analyzed and categorized by the quantitative analysis software “Igor” to obtain histogram plots of sizes. The resulting ZrF MOFs were characterized using an X-ray diffractometer (Bruker D8 Discover, USA) to determine their crystalline structures. Functional groups of ZrF MOFs were identified using FT-IR (Perkin Elmer Spectrum Two, USA). The textural properties of ZrF MOFs were measured using a gas volumetric analyzer (Quantachrome autosorb IQ, USA). The acidity of ZrF MOFs was determined by temperature-programmed desorption (TPD) - $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{3}$ (Quantachrome autosorb iQ-C-MP, USA). The fraction of Zr content in $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZrF}}$ was determined using inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) (Perkin Elmer Optima, USA) after digesting ZrF samples using ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ .
## 2.3 Catalytic conversion of LA to GVL
The conversion of LA to GVL via CTH was tested by batch-type solvothermal reactions. In a typical solvothermal experiment, 0.5 mmol of LA was added to $9.95~\mathrm{ml}$ of an alcohol (e.g., 2-PrOH) and $\boldsymbol{0.1\ \mathrm{g}}$ of $Z\mathrm{rF}$ was then charged into a $20\mathrm{-ml}$ stainless steel autoclave. The LA amount and catalyst dosage employed here were particularly selected based on typical values reported in literatures of LA conversion [13, 19, 30-32].
Next, ${\bf N}_{2}$ gas at 10 atm was introduced into the reactor, which was then placed inside a pre-heated conventional oven to reach a desired temperature. In the end of the experiment, catalysts were separated from reaction mixtures using syringe filters (0.22 $\upmu\mathrm{m})$ and products were analyzed using a gas chromatograph (Shimadzu GC-2030, Japan) with a frame ionization detector (FID) and liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS). In general, each experiment was repeated at least two times to obtain averages and standard deviations as error bars. LA conversion efficiency is typically quantified by three indices, including LA conversion $(\mathrm{C_{LA}})$ , yield for $\mathrm{GVL}\left(\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}\right)$ and selectivity for GVL $(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{GVL}})$ , which were calculated as follows [33]:
$$
C o n\nu e r s i o n(\mathcal{U})(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{LA}})=\frac{\mathbf{C}\mathrm{onsumed}\ L\mathbf{A}\left(\mathrm{mol}\right)}{\mathrm{Initial}\ L\mathbf{A}\left(\mathrm{mol}\right)}\times100\
$$
$$
\scriptstyle\gamma(\mathcal{I}_{o})(\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{GVL}})=\frac{\mathrm{GVL}\left(\mathrm{mol}\right)}{\mathrm{ConsumedLA}\left(\mathrm{mol}\right)}\times100\
$$
$$
Y i e l d(\mathcal{Y}_{O})(\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}){=}\frac{\mathrm{GVL~(mol)}}{\mathrm{Initial}\mathrm{LA(mol)}}{\times}100\
$$
3. Results and discussions:
## 3.1 Characterizations of ZrF MOFs
The usage of monocarboxylic acid modulators has been validated to influence morphologies of ZrF MOFs, especially their sizes and shapes [27]. When a modulator (e.g., monocarboxylic acid) is employed for preparing ZrF, the modulator with only one functional group would compete with the ligand (i.e., fumarate ion) for coordination sites of Zr. Since the modulator has only one functional group, it cannot bridge $Z\mathbf{r}$ ions to form frameworks [28, 34]. Nonetheless, the modulator would temporarily block the coordination sites to prevent unfavorable rapid nucleation and growth [28, 35]. Subsequently, the ligand (i.e., fumarate ion) would then gradually exchange with the modulator, leading to the formation of ZrF. The above-mentioned competition and blocking effect from the modulator, also called “the modulation effect”, can significantly reduce nucleation and growth rates, enabling the correct formation of ZrF [28].
Moreover, it has been found that the modulation effect greatly influenced size and shape of water-born ZrF [28]. Specifically, when more modulators and shorter modulators were employed, the resulting ZrF could be significantly larger and more non-spherical. In contrast, few modulators and longer modulators were employed, the resulting ZrF exhibited very small size and seriously aggregated [28].
Therefore, the morphologies of ZrF prepared using various modulators (e.g., formic acid, acetic acid and propanoic acid) were first characterized and shown in Fig. 1(a), (b) and (c), respectively. The size of ZrF has been correlated to molecular weights of monocarboxylic acids. The formic acid-modulated ZrF (F-ZrF) was found to be the largest, whereas A-ZrF was relatively smaller and P-ZrF seemed the smallest. The order was consistent to the previous study [27]. The size distributions of these modulated ZrF MOFs can be seen in Fig. 2(a), (b) and (c), correspondingly. The dominant size of F-ZrF was ca. $270~\mathrm{nm}$ , $130~\mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{F}$ and $55\ \mathrm{nm}$ for P-ZrF, validating that the sizes of ZrF MOFs were significantly influenced by the modulators and a modulator with a relatively low molecular weight led to a larger ZrF [27].
As three modulators led to quite distinct morphologies (sizes and shapes) of ZrF, different degrees of dispersion (or aggregation) of these ZrF materials in reaction media would be also expected to influence their catalytic performances for LA conversion to GVL.
While the modulators were proven to facilitate the formation of ZrF, it would be interesting to prepare a water-born ZrF without using modulators. Thus, the water-born ZrF (W-ZrF) without addition of monocarboxylic acids was correspondingly prepared as shown in Fig. 1(d). Nevertheless, the morphology of W-ZrF was totally distinct to the modulated ZrF MOFs; W-ZrF was a huge chunk and no granular particles were observed. This morphology was in line with a previous study that also observed non-patterned huge chunks for W-ZrF without modulators [27].
On the other hand, Fig. 1(e) shows the SEM image of D-ZrF which seemed to consist of many fine particles even smaller than P-ZrF. The size distribution (Fig. 2(d)) demonstrates that its dominant size was ca. 25 nm. In comparison with the non-patterned W-ZrF, the granular morphology of D-ZrF also demonstrates that solvents played an important for the formation of ZrF.
As Fig. 2(e) summarizes the size distributions of water-born modulated ZrF MOFs and D-ZrF, the sizes of these ZrF MOFs were certainly quite different and F-ZrF was the largest, followed by A-ZrF, P-ZrF and finally D-ZrF.
While the modulators and solvents significantly influenced the morphologies of ZrF MOFs, it was critical to verify their crystalline structures. Thus, XRD patterns of these ZrF MOFs were measured and shown in Fig. 3(a). Interestingly, even though different monocarboxylic acid modulators were employed, these water-born modulated ZrF exhibited the same XRD pattern which can be also well-indexed to the typical XRD pattern of ZrF [27]. This validates that regardless of modulators and their molecular weights, water-born modulated ZrF could be well-developed. Nevertheless, the water-born non-modulated ZrF (W-ZrF), although seeming to exhibit a few characteristic peaks of ZrF, was obviously not well-developed. This indicates that when water was used as a solvent to prepare ZrF, modulators are certainly necessitated for well-developed formation of ZrF.
On the other hand, Fig. 3(a) also includes the XRD pattern of the typical DMF-used ZrF, which exhibited the same XRD pattern to those of water-born modulated ZrF MOFs. This further validates that DMF-based solvents must be employed for preparing ZrF if no modulators were used.
In addition, the zirconium contents in these ZrF materials were also correspondingly analyzed using ICP-OES. The zirconium contents of F-ZrF, A-ZrF, P-ZrF, D-ZrF and W-ZrF are $38.0\%$ , $39.0\%$ , $39.6\%$ , $40.3\%$ and $49.4\%$ , respectively. As the reported formula of typical ZrF is $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ (fumarate)6 and the corresponding percentage of zirconium would then be $39.8\%$ . This indicates that F-ZrF, A-ZrF, P-ZrF and D-ZrF were well and correctly developed to contain comparable zirconium amounts to that of the typical ZrF. However, W-ZrF consisted of excess zirconium content. This was probably because Zr and fumarate ions nucleated too fast in the disordered structure during the preparation of W-ZrF in the absence of modulators, leading to the incorrect formation.
Moreover, Fig. 3(b) reveals the IR spectra of these ZrF MOFs. Irrespective of modulators, these water-born modulated ZrF MOFs all exhibited characteristic IR peaks reported in literatures [29]. In comparison to these water-born modulated ZrF, W-ZrF also exhibited a few signature IR peaks of ZrF; nevertheless the overall spectrum of W-ZrF was inconsistent with the reported IR spectrum of ZrF. This further confirms that the water-born non-modulated ZrF was non-fully developed. As the function of modulators is to facilitate the formation of the coordination structure established between $Z\mathbf{r}$ and fumarate, W-ZrF, without modulators, might contain other combinations of $\mathrm{zr}$ and fumarate different from the typical ZrF. Fig. 3(b) also reveals the IR spectrum of D-ZrF, which was consistent to that the reported ZrF pattern as for the water-born modulated ZrF MOFs. These IR spectra also validate that water-born ZrF must be prepared with assistance of modulators; otherwise DMF should be required for preparing ZrF.
Even though the water-born modulated ZrF MOFs revealed very comparable IR spectra, a close view of the spectrum of P-ZrF seemed to exhibit a number of short peaks which were not observed in other ZrF MOFs, such as the most typical D-ZrF. When the spectrum of P-ZrF was listed with the spectrum of its modulator, propanoic acid, as shown in Fig. S1, two very short peaks at 1074 and $1300~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be found in the spectrum of P-ZrF and these short peaks seemed attributed to its modulator. Nevertheless, in the cases of F-ZrF and A-ZrF, their IR spectra did not reveal significant additional peaks originated from their corresponding modulators as shown in Fig. S1(a) and (b). This suggests that there seemed to be some residues of the modulator, propanoic acid, remaining in P-ZrF, possibly because the relatively large propanoic acid might be less easily exchanged or replaced during the formation of ZrF.
As modulators and solvents significantly affected morphologies, textural properties of these ZrF, as essential characteristics for catalytic reactions, were also investigated as shown in Fig. 4(a). Based on the SEM images and size distributions, the sizes of water-born modulated ZrF MOFs were hugely distinct. Nevertheless, ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption/desorption isotherms of these three modulated ZrF were quite similar. The corresponding isotherms can be classified as the IUPAC type I isotherm, indicating that these water-born modulated ZrF were highly porous. However, P-ZrF still
ZrF MOFs; and (c) thermogravimetric analyses of water-born modulated $Z\mathrm{rF}$ . exhibited a relatively high surface area of $940~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , whereas A-ZrF showed a surface area of $890~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and the surface area of $\mathrm{F}{-}\mathrm{ZrF}$ was $830~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . Meanwhile, surface areas of D-ZrF and W-ZrF were also measured and determined as $410~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and 15 $\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively, indicating that non-modulated water-born ZrF was an aggregate with a very low surface area. A previous study had also validated that non-modulated
ZrF seemed to exhibit very low surface areas even though DMF-based solvents were used for preparing ZrF [29]. This was possibly because the D-ZrF particle was too small and seriously aggregated, leading to the relatively low surface area.
In addition to the textural properties via analyzing ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms of these ZrF MOFs, it was also critical to investigate acidities of these ZrF MOFs especially as acidity of catalysts for LA conversion plays an important role for conversion of LA to GVL which necessarily involves dehydration [32]. To this end, TPD- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{3}$ analyses of these ZrF MOFs were conducted as quantities and intensities of Lewis/Bronsted acid sites can be determined by TPD- $\mathrm{.NH}_{3}$ because $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , smaller than pyridine, is expected to react with most of acid sites, such as electron accepting sites and hydrogen of hydroxyls [36]. Fig. 4(b) shows TPD- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ profiles of these ZrF MOFs in the temperature range from 80 to 280 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Interestingly, even though the water-born modulated ZrF MOFs exhibited distinct morphologies, their profiles of TPD- $\mathrm{.NH}_{3}$ in fact were very comparable in terms of temperature ranges and TCD signals. This further demonstrates that the acidities of these modulated ZrF MOFs remained almost the same even though different carboxylic acid modulators were employed during syntheses. Acidities of modulated ZrF MOFs could be attributed to Zr-O clusters formed between Zr and oxygenic groups of fumaric acid [14, 20]. Therefore, the comparable acidities of these modulators indicate that these Zr-O clusters were not significantly changed even though different modulators were used.
On the other hand, if no modulators were used, the pristine water-born ZrF (W-ZrF) revealed a very low amount of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ desorbed while its range temperature lay within the temperature range of the modulated $Z\mathrm{rF}$ . This suggests that W-ZrF seemed to exhibit a very low extent of acidity which might be attributed to very few Zr-O clusters. Nevertheless, this extreme low acidity validates that Zr was not correctly bound to the oxygenic group of furmaric acid, thereby leading to the inappropriate XRD pattern as shown in Fig. 3(a).
Fig. 4(b) also shows the TPD- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{3}$ profile of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{\mathrm{D-ZrF}}.$ , which actually was quite similar to those of the modulated ZrF MOFs. Nevertheless, the amount of NH3 desorbed seemed slightly less than that of the modulated ZrF, suggesting that the acidity of $Z\mathrm{rF}$ prepared in water with modulators was not diminished.
Since the water-born modulated ZrF were particularly focused on here, their thermogravimetric analyses were also performed as shown in Fig. 4(a). While these three ZrF exhibited quite different morphologies but similar XRD/IR features, their profiles of derivative TG (DTG) were quite similar with a significant weight loss at around $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , possibly due to adsorbed gas/moisture, followed by another huge weight loss before $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ due to decomposition of the ligand. Around $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ another weight loss can be also observed and probably attributed to decomposition of ZrF. Interestingly, even though the weight losses of these three $Z\mathrm{rF}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 390 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were almost identical, the weight losses at around $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were slightly different. F-ZrF seemed to reveal the weight loss at a relatively low temperature, followed by A-ZrF and P-ZrF. This suggests that these three water-born modulated ZrF essentially exhibited very similar DTG profiles but their TG behaviors were not completely identical, possibly because of the distinct morphologies owing to modulators.
3.2 LA conversion to GVL using various ZrF MOFs at different temperatures As temperature plays important role for LA conversion, LA conversion to GVL using these ZrF MOFs was first conducted at different temperatures, ranging from 160 to $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Fig. 5(a) shows LA conversion using the modulated F-ZrF; F-ZrF was able to exhibit $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{LA}}}=85$ , $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{LA}}}=80$ and $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}=74\%$ at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , indicating that the water-born (a) F-ZrF, (b) A-ZrF, (c) P-ZrF, (d) D-ZrF and (e) W-ZrF (the reaction time $=2$ hours).
modulated was certainly capable of converting LA to GVL. When the temperature increased, LA conversion, selectivity for GVL and yield of GVL using F-ZrF also grew noticeably and reached $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{LA}}}=96\%$ , $\mathrm{S_{GVL}}=98\%$ and $\mathrm{Y_{GVL}}=98\%$ at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . This further demonstrates that LA could be almost completely converted to GVL using the water-born modulated $Z\mathrm{rF}$ at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . On the other hand, Fig. 5(b) reveals the LA conversion using another modulated A-ZrF. At $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , A-ZrF was able to reach $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{LA}}}=90\%$ , $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}_{\mathrm{GVL}}=70\%$ and $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}=60\%$ . When the reaction temperature increased, the LA conversion, selectivity for GVL and yield of GVL were also significantly improved, reaching $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{LA}}}=90\%$ , $\mathrm{S_{GVL}}=87\%$ and $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}=90\%$ at $200\mathrm{oC}.$ The similar trend can be observed for the third modulated ZrF, P-ZrF, which exhibited $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{LA}}}=63\%$ , $\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{GVL}}=57\%$ and $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}=27\%$ at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and became $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{LA}}}=80\%$ , $\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{GVL}}=82\%$ and $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}=95\%$ at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . These results validate that the water-born ZrF was certainly able to transform LA to GVL and YGVL by three modulated $Z\mathrm{rF}$ all exceeded $90\%$ at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , indicating that these water-born modulated $Z\mathrm{rF}$ were highly catalytically active for LA conversion to GVL.
Typically, when Lewis acid and Brønsted acid sites are both present, Lewis acid sites are typically responsible for conversion of LA to 4-HPA, whereas Brønsted acid sites can further convert the resulting 4-HPA to GVL[33, 37, 38]. However, according to literatures [39, 40], the amphoteric nature of the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based catalysts comprised of Zr-O clusters was the major cause for their high activity because both acidic and basic sites of catalysts are crucial for a CTH reaction. The acid sites mainly originate from $\boldsymbol{Z\mathrm{r}}^{4+}$ and the basic sites result from $0^{2\cdot}$ . The origin of the acid-base sites was developed from $Z\mathrm{r}{-}\mathrm{O}{-}Z\mathrm{r}$ or $Z\mathrm{r-OH-Zr}$ bonding present in the metal clusters (i.e., Zr-O) in the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs. Therefore, a mechanism of LA conversion to GVL via CTH using ZrF can be correspondingly proposed as follows [39, 41, 42]: the adsorption of IPA first appears on the $Z\mathbf{r}$ -O cluster of ZrF and subsequently dissociate to the corresponding alkoxide and hydrogen by the unsaturated $\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}{-\mathrm{O}^{2-}}$ acid-base pair sites, in which the deprotonation of alcohol is promoted by the basic property of the carboxylate $0^{2\cdot}$ atoms.
Besides, the surface-adsorbed alkoxide transfers a hydride ion that attacks the carbonyl group of LA subsequently activated by acidic ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ sites to yield 4-HPA for second step. Next, 4-HPA is formed by hydrogen transfer in the third step reaction between the dissociated alkoxide and the active LA via the formation of a six-membered ring transition state. The formed 4-HPA and carbonyl byproduct are then released to regenerate the initial active sites as the fourth step 4. Finally, 4-HPA is converted to GVL via the intramolecular dealcoholization to accomplish the final step [39, 41, 42].
Even though these modulated ZrF were proven to successfully convert LA to GVL and reach quite high conversions and yields at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , it was interesting that these modulated ZrF MOFs exhibited different efficiencies of LA conversion at relatively low temperatures (e.g., $160{-}180\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . As these three modulated ZrF MOFs were essentially similar in terms of crystalline structures, and acidities, these differences might be attributed to the hugely distinct morphologies. As discussed in the earlier section, it has been reported that $Z\mathrm{rF}$ modulated by monocarboxylic acids with long alkyl chains appeared to be relatively small in size and prone to aggregating [28]. Therefore, the relatively small ZrF (e.g., P-ZrF) certainly seemed bundled together as shown in Fig. 1, and the aggregation of P-ZrF might diminish its catalytic activities, leading the less effective LA conversion efficiencies.
Furthermore, D-ZrF was also tested for LA conversion at different temperatures for comparisons with the modulated ZrF MOFs as shown in Fig. 5(d). At $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , D-ZrF could reach $\mathrm{CLA}=80\%$ , $\mathrm{SGVL}=60\%$ and $\mathrm{\YGVL}=32\%$ , which were improved to $\mathbf{CLA}=90\%$ , $\mathrm{SGVL}=80\%$ and $\mathrm{YGVL}=78\%$ at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , validating the positive effect of elevated temperatures. In comparison with the modulated ZrF MOFs, D-ZrF in fact did not surpass the water-born modulated ZrF in LA conversion efficiencies. Instead, these water-born modulated ZrF MOFs all exhibited higher YGVL and SGVL than D-ZrF at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . F-ZrF and A-ZrF even outperformed D-ZrF in LA conversion to GVL at relatively low temperatures.
These results confirm that the water-born modulated ZrF were not only feasible for LA conversion to GVL, but also exhibited higher catalytic activities than D-ZrF for LA conversion. This might be attributed to the considerably lower surface area and slightly lower acidity of D-ZrF than those modulated ZrF MOFs.
This also demonstrates that ZrF prepared just in water in the presence of modulators was not only more environmentally benign but also more effective for LA conversion to GVL. Nevertheless, as Fig. 5(e) reveals that the water-born non-modulated ZrF, even though was still useful for LA conversion to GVL, was inefficient possibly due to its very low surface area and acidity. These results prove that the role of the modulators for LA conversion using water-born ZrF MOFs was exceedingly critical for affording the correct crystalline structure of ZrF, large surface areas and sufficient acidities to convert LA to GVL.
