## 3.3 Effect of reaction time on LA conversion using water-born modulated ZrF
As this study was focused on evaluating water-born ZrF for LA conversion to GVL, LA conversion efficiencies as a function of time by these three modulated ZrF MOFs were further determined. According to the effect of temperature on LA conversion, $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ seemed as a satisfactory temperature for LA conversion using $Z\mathrm{rF}$ . Thus, 200 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was selected and fixed while the reaction time was changed from 0.5 to 2.0 hours. In the case of F-ZrF (Fig. 6(a)), when the reaction time was only 0.5 hour, F-ZrF was still capable of converting LA to GVL with $C_{\mathrm{LA}}=95\%$ and $\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{GVL}}=90\%$ but $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}$ was only $32\%$ . This suggests that LA might be converted to intermediates, which could not be successfully converted to the target products within this short period of time. Thus, when the reaction time increased to 1.0 hour, $\mathrm{C_{LA}}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{GVL}}$ remained comparable but $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}$ considerably increased to $60\%$ , which was furthered to 70 and $95\ \%$ at 1.5 and 2.0 hours, respectively. This indicates that a sufficient reaction time was critical for the successful conversion of LA to GVL using F-ZrF.
Similar features can be also observed for LA conversion at different times using A-ZrF (Fig. 6(b)), which exhibited gradually increased $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}$ over 2 hours while maintaining $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{LA}}$ and $\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{GVL}}$ . In the case of P-ZrF (Fig. 6(c)), even though the similar trend was noted, relatively $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}$ was achieved within shorter times. For instance, the corresponding $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}$ at 0.5 and 1 hour had reached $>$ $60\%$ . This suggests that the reaction kinetics of LA conversion by P-ZrF seemed slightly faster than other modulated ZrF, possibly because P-ZrF exhibited a relatively high surface which afforded more accessible active sites for LA conversion. Overall, the reaction time appears as another critical parameter for modulated ZrF in LA conversion, especially in the cases of F-ZrF and A-ZrF.
As some other products might be generated from LA conversion, the intermediates generated from LA conversion via CTH as a function of reaction time were determined using LC-MS/MS and GC as shown in Fig. S2. In addition to GVL, two intermediates were present during LA conversion: isopropyl levulinate (IPL) and 4-hydroxypentanoic acid (4-HPA). IPL was derived from transesterification of LA, which could then undergo further CTH to yield GVL. On the other hand, 4-HPA, generated by hydrogenation of LA, underwent a close-ring reaction by intramolecular esterification-dehydration to form GVL [43, 44]. These production distributions demonstrate that while the intermediates, 4-HPA and IPL, could be detected, their amounts were relatively low. The major product of LA conversion was certainly GVL.
## 3.4 LA conversion using water-born ZrF in various alcohols
As alcohols are essential for CTH reactions as H-donors, several common alcohols were evaluated for LA conversion to GVL via CTH, including methanol, ethanol, 1-propanol, 2-propanol, 1-butanol and 2-butanol. Fig. 7(a) first reveals LA conversion efficiencies catalyzed by F-ZrF in various alcohols.
When methanol was employed, GVL was barely obtained, indicating that methanol was infeasible for converting LA to GVL [14, 15, 33]. Even though ethanol and 1-propanol were used and the conversion efficiencies seemed improved, the corresponding YGVL was still quite low with a very low selectivity. This was because these primary alcohols are relatively difficult for the $\beta$ -hydride elimination from alkoxy intermediates during CTH processes [16]. In contrast, LA conversion efficiencies by F-ZrF were noticeably improved using secondary alcohols, such as 2- propanol and 2- butanol, because these secondary alcohols are relatively easy for the $\beta$ -hydride elimination to generate GVL [23].
On the other hand, LA conversion efficiencies by A-ZrF and P-ZrF also revealed similar trends and LA conversion in the secondary alcohols were much more effective than the primary alcohols. Particularly, 2-butanol seemed also a promising alcohol for LA conversion to GVL by these ZrF MOFs.
## 3.5 Conversion of various levulinate esters to GVL using water-born ZrF
In addition to LA, levulinate esters (LAEs) are considered as alternative substrates for producing GVL. The result of converting LA and its esters by F-ZrF, such as methyl levulinate (ML), ethyl levulinate (EL) and butyl levulinate (BL) to GVL are shown in Fig. 8(a). The conversion efficiency of LA to GVL appeared to be the highest owing to the fact that the acidity of LA can accelerate the lactonization of 4-HPA to afford GVL [45]. According to previous findings [14], hydrophobicity and steric hindrance of levulinate esters are the critical parameters that influence GVL production. Substrates with elongated alkyl chains result in higher hydrophobicity, which seriously affects dealcoholization during GVL formation. As a result, the conversion efficiency of BL to GVL was relatively low due to its more hydrophobic longer alkyl chain [14]. On the contrary, since ML and EL with less hydrophobic characteristics, their corresponding conversion efficiencies were relatively high in comparison to that of BL.
On the other hand, A-ZrF and P-ZrF were also found to convert these various LAEs to GVL, validating that these water-born modulated ZrF can be also applied in conversion of LAEs. In addition, the effect of LAE species on conversion to GVL was similar and LA conversion efficiencies to GVL remained as the highest, followed by ML, EL and finally BL.
## 3.6 Reusability of water-born ZrF for LA conversion to GVL
While these water-born modulated ZrF exhibited promisingly effective catalytic activities for LA conversion to GVL, it was important to investigate whether ZrF could be reused for LA conversion to GVL over multiple times. Therefore, multi-cyclic tests of LA conversion using used ZrF MOFs were performed and the result is shown in Fig. 9. Fig. 9(a) shows a consecutive 6 cycles of LA conversion using used F-ZrF, which was not regenerated or cleaned additionally.
As revealed in Fig. 9(a), the used $Z\mathrm{rF}$ was still capable of converting LA to GVL. Even though slight decreases in $\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{GVL}}$ and $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}$ could be observed, possibly due to impurities residing on the surface of $\mathrm{F}{-}\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{F}$ , the used F-ZrF could still exhibit very stable and satisfactory conversion efficiencies over 5 additional cycles without significant losses. In the cases of A-ZrF and P-ZrF, similar results can be also obtained, validating that these water-born modulated ZrF can be reused and their catalytic activities were highly stable.
## Conclusions
In this study, the role of modulators was found to be extremely important for the correct and successful formation of crystalline structure of ZrF. More importantly, different monocarboxylic acid modulators significantly influenced the sizes and shapes of water-born modulated ZrF. As for LA conversion, the large-sized ZrF modulated by formic acid was more favorable than small-sized ZrF modulated by propanoic acid for LA conversion to GVL. Additionally, these water-born modulated ZrF could exhibit even higher catalytic activities than the typical DMF-based ZrF in LA conversion to GVL. This demonstrates that the water-born ZrF was not only more environmentally benign but also more effective than D-ZrF for LA conversion to GVL. In comparison to UiO-66-SO3H, which was prepared in DMF, the water-born could also reveal a considerably higher LA conversion efficiency to GVL with $\mathrm{C_{LA}}$ of $96\%$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{GVL}}$ of 98 and $\mathrm{Y}_{\mathrm{GVL}}$ of $98\%$ . These water-born ZrF were also capable of converting LAEs to GVL, revealing their versatility and applicability. Finally, these water-born ZrF could be also reused even without regeneration and cleaning for multiple cyclic LA conversion. These results indicate that the modulation approach is not only useful for controlling the preparation but also advantageous for LA conversion efficiency. The findings shown here are also valuable for probing into the effects of modulators on physical and catalytic properties of MOFs.
